Hi rtCamp team,

We sent you a client security audit for aljazeera.com with a published grade =
of F (40/100). That grade was wrong. Our scoring model at the time penalised =
every finding flatly and counted best-practice configuration items as vulnera=
bilities. Re-scoring the same scan under the corrected model gives B (80/100).

Three finding classes drove the wrong grade: the SSL/TLS certificate expiring=
 soon, the missing HTTP response hardening headers (CSP, X-Frame-Options, X-C=
ontent-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy, Permissions-Policy, COOP) and the missi=
ng mail security records (BIMI, MTA-STS, TLS-RPT).

One critical or high finding in that report is unaffected by this correction =
and still needs attention.

The rule now: best-practice items are reported but never scored, a scan with =
no critical or high finding cannot score below 60, and repeated findings of t=
he same class are charged at a reduced rate under a per-class cap.

Next step: reply to this note and we will re-issue the aljazeera.com audit wi=
th the corrected grade and score. No change to the client's configuration is =
required to correct the score.

Best regards,
Carson Haynes
Founder, Orbit Security
carsonmail009@gmail.com
https://cmfh009.github.io/Orbit-Security/

What changed:

- Best-practice items (BIMI, MTA-STS, TLS-RPT, CAA, incomplete HSTS, CSP, X-F=
rame-Options, X-Content-Type-Options, Referrer-Policy, Permissions-Policy, CO=
OP, security.txt) are still detected and still reported, but they are no long=
er scored as vulnerabilities.
- A scan with no critical and no high finding cannot score below 60 (grade C)=
, so a failing grade always means exploitable risk.
- Repeated findings of the same class are charged at a reduced rate and each =
class is capped, so a large estate is not punished for being large.
- Grade bands are unchanged: A+ 95, A 88, B 75, C 60, D 45, F below 45.
